    "result_text": "All tests pass. `AcceptanceTest` is the project's only test class, and the full `mvn test` build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI redesigned the identity key with minimal structural impact \u2014 **3 files**, each change small and confined to a low-complexity unit:\n\n1. **New `Soundex.java`** \u2014 a self-contained American Soundex encoder (`encode(name)` \u2192 letter + 3 digits). Isolating it in its own class keeps its inherent complexity away from the classes that consume it, so it adds no `WMC_other` burden to any edited method.\n\n2. **`OwnerIdentity.key`** \u2014 now returns `SHA-256 hex over (normalizedTelephone + '|' + lowerEmail + '|' + soundex(lastName))`. Since both the 409 check (`EnsureUniqueIdentity`) and the GET-time DTO field (`OwnerMapper`) already route through this single method, duplicate detection and the exposed `identityKey` updated automatically \u2014 no changes needed there. Blocklist (`RejectDisposableEmail`) still runs earlier in the pipeline; deleted owners are still skipped.\n\n3. **`FlagPossibleDuplicate.softMatches`** \u2014 the soft match now triggers when `soundex(lastName)` **and** `postcode` match but the `identityKey` differs. Two owners with the same last name + postcode but different telephones now produce different keys, so they're flagged as a soft match instead of rejected.\n\nThe old household-duplicate 409 block (`EnsureUniqueHousehold`) was already dead code \u2014 not wired into any YAML \u2014 so the \"no longer applies\" requirement needed no removal. `AssignHousehold` still computes `householdId` (used elsewhere) and was left untouched.\n\nVerified behaviors:\n- `coreIdentityKeyIsSha256Hex` \u2014 identityKey matches `[0-9a-f]{64}` \u2713\n- `coreRejectsDuplicateIdentity` \u2014 identical telephone/email/lastName \u2192 409 \u2713",
